1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,020
Your dashboard can be correct. Your code can

2
00:00:03,020 --> 00:00:06,660
run perfectly. Your model can converge. And your

3
00:00:06,660 --> 00:00:09,360
answer can still be wrong. Imagine ten thousand

4
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:12,500
reported records. Are they really ten thousand different

5
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:15,440
people? The problem may have started before the

6
00:00:15,440 --> 00:00:16,290
analysis began.

7
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:19,730
Meet Amina, a synthetic person in our example.

8
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:22,660
She registers at Clinic A, then moves and

9
00:00:22,660 --> 00:00:25,320
registers at Clinic B. One record says Amina

10
00:00:25,320 --> 00:00:28,040
K. Another uses capital letters. Her date of

11
00:00:28,040 --> 00:00:30,180
birth and phone number look different because the

12
00:00:30,180 --> 00:00:33,480
formats changed. Two records do not necessarily mean

13
00:00:33,480 --> 00:00:37,440
two people. Mobility, re-registration and disconnected systems can

14
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:38,650
all create this problem.

15
00:00:39,360 --> 00:00:42,820
Suppose adjudication establishes nine thousand two hundred unique

16
00:00:42,820 --> 00:00:45,618
people among ten thousand records. That leaves eight

17
00:00:45,618 --> 00:00:48,344
hundred extra records. Divide eight hundred by nine

18
00:00:48,344 --> 00:00:51,240
thousand two hundred: the apparent overcount is about

19
00:00:51,240 --> 00:00:55,539
eight point seven percent. The denominator matters. Inflated

20
00:00:55,539 --> 00:00:58,879
counts can distort coverage, resource allocation and downstream

21
00:00:58,879 --> 00:01:02,999
analysis. Correct arithmetic cannot repair a mistaken unit

22
00:01:02,999 --> 00:01:03,429
of counting.

23
00:01:03,919 --> 00:01:05,439
Over the years, I have used a practical

24
00:01:05,439 --> 00:01:08,369
data-quality memory framework: V I P, R T.

25
00:01:09,179 --> 00:01:13,559
Validity. Integrity. Precision. Reliability. Timeliness. This is my

26
00:01:13,559 --> 00:01:17,239
memory aid, rather than a universal taxonomy. Deduplication

27
00:01:17,239 --> 00:01:20,399
can affect several dimensions. Today, the question is

28
00:01:20,399 --> 00:01:23,119
whether two records represent two entities, or one

29
00:01:23,119 --> 00:01:24,329
entity represented twice.

30
00:01:24,629 --> 00:01:28,289
When a reliable unique identifier is unavailable, combine

31
00:01:28,289 --> 00:01:32,149
selected, standardized attributes into a composite key. Our

32
00:01:32,149 --> 00:01:34,949
teaching example uses date of birth, sex and

33
00:01:34,949 --> 00:01:39,309
district. First, standardize capitalization and formats. Then build

34
00:01:39,309 --> 00:01:41,249
the key and use it to form candidate

35
00:01:41,249 --> 00:01:44,349
groups. Select fields with stakeholders under an approved

36
00:01:44,349 --> 00:01:47,176
protocol. An identical key can occur for different

37
00:01:47,176 --> 00:01:49,696
people. A missing or changed district can also

38
00:01:49,696 --> 00:01:52,566
separate records that really belong together.

39
00:01:52,866 --> 00:01:56,456
The workflow starts with co-design. Standardize the records.

40
00:01:57,246 --> 00:01:59,966
Build the key. Block records to avoid comparing

41
00:01:59,966 --> 00:02:03,686
every possible pair. Compare exact and, where appropriate,

42
00:02:03,686 --> 00:02:07,946
fuzzy fields. Flag candidates. Then adjudicate using permitted

43
00:02:07,946 --> 00:02:11,886
source information. A name similarity of ninety-six percent

44
00:02:11,886 --> 00:02:14,766
is not a ninety-six percent probability that two

45
00:02:14,766 --> 00:02:18,146
people are the same. Review can establish a

46
00:02:18,146 --> 00:02:23,066
confirmed duplicate, or separate people. Resolve only supported

47
00:02:23,066 --> 00:02:26,366
links and preserve an audit trail. A match

48
00:02:26,366 --> 00:02:28,196
is a flag, not a verdict.

49
00:02:28,668 --> 00:02:31,428
There is no magic threshold. Lowering it can

50
00:02:31,428 --> 00:02:34,058
capture more duplicates while creating more false matches.

51
00:02:34,868 --> 00:02:38,348
Raising it can miss genuine duplicates. Validate precision

52
00:02:38,348 --> 00:02:41,428
and recall for your setting. Also measure what

53
00:02:41,428 --> 00:02:44,468
blocking missed. In our synthetic notebook, the key

54
00:02:44,468 --> 00:02:47,888
retains five hundred fifty-two of six hundred true

55
00:02:47,888 --> 00:02:53,968
duplicate pairs: ninety-two percent blocking recall. Candidate-pair recall

56
00:02:53,968 --> 00:02:56,998
alone hides those forty-eight missed pairs.

57
00:02:57,078 --> 00:03:01,468
Reliable, governed unique identifiers can support exact matching.

58
00:03:01,938 --> 00:03:05,718
Composite-key and probabilistic approaches can generate candidates where

59
00:03:05,718 --> 00:03:10,158
reliable identifiers are unavailable. Neither national identifiers nor

60
00:03:10,158 --> 00:03:14,978
biometrics are infallible. Missing, erroneous or incorrectly linked

61
00:03:14,978 --> 00:03:19,358
identifiers still need quality checks. Real-world linkage requires

62
00:03:19,358 --> 00:03:23,658
an approved protocol, privacy safeguards, minimum-necessary information,

63
00:03:23,658 --> 00:03:28,358
secure processing, appropriate ethics and governance review, and

64
00:03:28,358 --> 00:03:29,848
documented adjudication.

65
00:03:30,718 --> 00:03:34,118
The same question reaches far beyond health. A

66
00:03:34,118 --> 00:03:37,158
student transfers schools. A customer joins through two

67
00:03:37,158 --> 00:03:40,628
channels. A beneficiary appears on multiple assistance lists.

68
00:03:41,358 --> 00:03:44,738
One shopper creates several accounts. Government records span

69
00:03:44,738 --> 00:03:48,618
disconnected systems. In each case, decide what entity

70
00:03:48,618 --> 00:03:50,988
you are counting before you interpret the total.

71
00:03:51,203 --> 00:03:53,536
Duplicates can change the weight that particular people

72
00:03:53,536 --> 00:03:56,886
receive in an analysis. That can shift distributions,

73
00:03:57,396 --> 00:04:00,881
coefficient estimates, uncertainty and model behavior. The notebook

74
00:04:00,881 --> 00:04:04,121
compares duplicated records with an oracle benchmark built

75
00:04:04,121 --> 00:04:07,071
from known synthetic truth. That benchmark is not

76
00:04:07,071 --> 00:04:10,571
proof the matching workflow found every duplicate. Its

77
00:04:10,571 --> 00:04:13,671
training A U C is an illustration, not

78
00:04:13,671 --> 00:04:15,521
a claim of predictive validation.

79
00:04:16,151 --> 00:04:20,331
Now run the EvidenceLab notebook. Generate five thousand

80
00:04:20,331 --> 00:04:23,381
synthetic people and five thousand six hundred records.

81
00:04:23,751 --> 00:04:27,911
Standardize fields, build the key, inspect candidates and

82
00:04:27,911 --> 00:04:31,627
compare thresholds. Watch end-to-end recall as well as

83
00:04:31,627 --> 00:04:35,017
precision. Keep the raw data and document decisions.

84
00:04:35,767 --> 00:04:37,987
Before you trust the model, make sure you

85
00:04:37,987 --> 00:04:40,367
can trust the records that built it. Better

86
00:04:40,367 --> 00:04:45,697
data. Better analysis. Better models. Better decisions. EvidenceLab.

87
00:04:46,087 --> 00:04:49,097
See it. Understand it. Run it.